{"causes":["Rumi's spiritual teachings and devotional poetry influenced the development of Sufism, a mystical tradition within Islam.","Rumi's masnavi was written during the reign of the Mongol Empire, a time of great cultural and political upheaval.","Rumi's poetry and teachings attracted a large and diverse following, including scholars, mystics, and common people from all walks of life."],"effects":["The Masnavi has been translated into many languages and has inspired countless adaptations in literature, music, and art.","The Masnavi has had a profound impact on Islamic mysticism, shaping the spiritual practices and devotional poetry of Sufis worldwide.","The Masnavi has influenced Western literature and culture, with its themes of love, spirituality, and social justice resonating with readers across the globe."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Rumi","sourceName":"Rumi","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Sufism","targetName":"Sufism","context":"Rumi's spiritual teachings and devotional poetry shaped the development of Sufism, a mystical tradition within Islam."},{"sourceSlug":"Mongol Empire","sourceName":"Mongol Empire","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Rumi's Masnavi","targetName":"Rumi's Masnavi","context":"Rumi's masnavi was written during the reign of the Mongol Empire, a time of great cultural and political upheaval."},{"sourceSlug":"Sufism","sourceName":"Sufism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Western Literature","targetName":"Western Literature","context":"The Masnavi has influenced Western literature and culture, with its themes of love, spirituality, and social justice resonating with readers across the globe."},{"sourceSlug":"Rumi","sourceName":"Rumi","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"Western Literature","targetName":"Western Literature","context":"The Masnavi has had a profound impact on Islamic mysticism, shaping the spiritual practices and devotional poetry of Sufis worldwide."}],"places":[{"name":"Konya","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Mecca","role":"Place of Pilgrimage"},{"name":"Shiraz","role":"Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Masnavi","type":"Reference article","slug":"masnavi"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6579646","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masnavi"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Masnavi contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Masnavi (Persian: مثنوی معنوی, Maṡnavī-ye Maʿnavī, 'Spiritual Couplets') is a six-volume poem in rhyming couplets (masnavi metre) by the Persian Sufi poet and mystic Jalāl al-Dīn Muḥammad Rūmī (1207–1273), composed in Konya (in present-day Turkey) c. 1258–1273 at the instigation of his disciple Husam al-Din Chalabi. Running to approximately 25,000 couplets (51,000 lines) and one of the longest poems in any language, the Masnavi is the summit of Persian Sufi poetry and the work that earned Rumi the title 'Mawlana' (Our Master). It is sometimes called 'the Quran of the Persian language' — a reflection of its central position in Persian literary and spiritual culture.

The Masnavi's structure is associative rather than linear — it moves through stories, parables, Quranic commentary, philosophical discussions, and lyrical passages in a manner that enacts the Sufi understanding of spiritual reality as a web of interconnected meanings rather than a sequential argument. Its opening verses — the 'reed flute' (ney) passage — are among the most famous in all of Persian literature: the image of the reed flute's music as the cry of separation from its origin (the reed bed) as an allegory of the soul's longing for union with the divine is one of the defining metaphors of Sufi thought. The Masnavi contains dozens of embedded stories — many drawn from the Quran, Hadith, Persian folk tradition, and Hindu narratives — that are used as vehicles for Sufi teaching: the story of the reed flute, the elephant in the dark room (a parable for the divine beyond human comprehension), the three fishermen, the lion who hunts alone (on free will and divine help), and hundreds of others.

Rumi's Masnavi has been translated into dozens of languages and is among the best-selling poetry collections in the United States in the late 20th and early 21st centuries (in the translations of Coleman Barks, which, though free paraphrases, introduced millions of English-speaking readers to Sufi mystical poetry). It remains a foundational text of Persian literary culture and of the Sufi tradition worldwide.","subjects":["Persian Literature","Medieval Era","Rumi","Sufi Poetry","Islamic Mysticism","Persian Culture","Epic Poetry","Mystical Literature"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Masnavi has had a profound impact on Islamic mysticism and has influenced countless writers, artists, and musicians across the world. Its legacy extends beyond literature, shaping the cultural and spiritual landscape of the Middle East and beyond.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
